  (subsection public-release
   (doc "Hosted public face on the image: layout components, welcome, code browser, book PDF, source tarball")
   (status implemented)
   (implements
    libs/layout/lib.pp
    libs/welcome/lib.pp
    image/lib/http_code.ml
    image/lib/http_core.ml
    image/lib/http.ml
    image/lib/session.ml
    image/lib/agent_discovery.ml
    image/static/codemirror
    scripts/public_artifacts.sh)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "unauth GET /welcome returns 200 with hero")
    (smoke "unauth GET /code returns tree + codemirror shell")
    (smoke "unauth GET /code/raw/pricklypear.borg returns text")
    (smoke "unauth GET /book.pdf or 404 if artifact missing")
    (smoke "unauth GET /src.tgz or 404 if artifact missing")
    (smoke "path escape /code/raw/../etc/passwd denied")
    (smoke "unauth GET /bindings still redirects to login")
    (smoke "unauth GET /.well-known/agent.json returns 200 application/json with auth+eval contract")
    (smoke "unauth GET /agent.txt returns 200 text/plain agent instructions")
    (smoke "unauth GET /agent still denied (private portal)")
    (smoke "unauth POST /api/eval still denied"))

   (details
    (doc (|
         =========================================================
         GOAL
         =========================================================
         Release pricklypear on the hosted instance itself — not
         GitHub-as-homepage, not a sister brochure site. The live
         image IS the public site:

           /welcome   — landing (Nopales layout/* + welcome/page)
           /code      — read-only source browser (kernel + CM5)
           /book.pdf  — borge book print artifact
           /src.tgz   — pruned source tarball
           /.well-known/agent.json — machine agent bootstrap
           /agent.txt — prose agent instructions (auth + eval)
           /login     — enter the private workspace

         NOTE: /agent is the PRIVATE in-image agent portal (HTML),
         not the public discovery doc. Public agent onboarding is
         /.well-known/agent.json + /agent.txt only.

         /book (HTML explainer) is NOT public-facing — PDF + /code
         cover the literate book story. Any welcome/book page is
         private workspace chrome only, not a front-door path.

         Workspace home (/) stays HARD PRIVATE. Anon hitting /
         redirects to /welcome (or login with next=); logged-in
         users keep product home (ui/home).

         =========================================================
         LAYOUT COMPONENTS (libs/layout)
         =========================================================
         Namespace layout/* — NOT public/*.
         "public" is reserved for route visibility / gate language.

         Building blocks (pure HTML strings via html/*):
           layout/hero title tagline actions-html
           layout/card title body
           layout/cards list-of-card-html
           layout/section title body
           layout/cta href label
           layout/theme  — css/sheet rules for .layout-*

         Seeded on system/main; used by welcome (and private) pages.
         Personal gardens may override layout/* like any package.

         =========================================================
         WELCOME PAGE (libs/welcome)
         =========================================================
         welcome/page  — compose layout/* into landing
         (public-route "/welcome" "welcome/page")

         welcome/book may exist as a private helper, but MUST NOT be
         registered public and MUST NOT be a SYSTEM_HARD_PUBLIC path.
         Reseed should (route "/book" … 'private) or drop the binding
         so older public /book rows flip closed on deploy.

         Also register hard-public kernel paths so the front door
         works even if seed/route table is empty (belt + suspenders):
         /welcome is hard-public and dispatches to Nopales when bound,
         else a minimal kernel fallback page.

         CTAs: Explore source → /code ; Download book → /book.pdf ;
         Source tarball → /src.tgz ; Log in → /login.

         =========================================================
         CODE BROWSER (kernel, image/lib/http_code.ml)
         =========================================================
         Read-only filesystem browser over PP_SOURCE_ROOT
         (default: repo root / PP_ROOT / cwd with libs+image+lisp).

         Routes (all hard-public GET):
           /code              — HTML shell: file tree + CodeMirror
           /code?f=path       — open file in editor
           /code/raw/<path>   — raw text (for fetch into CM)
           /assets/cm/*       — vendored CodeMirror 5 assets

         CodeMirror 5 (vendored, no npm at runtime):
           codemirror.min.js + codemirror.min.css
           mode/mllike (text/x-ocaml for .ml/.mli)
           mode/markdown for .md
           plain for .borg .pp .sql .sh
           readOnly: true, lineNumbers: true

         SANDBOX:
           - realpath must stay under PP_SOURCE_ROOT
           - allowlist extensions: .borg .ml .mli .pp .md .sql
             .sh .ksh .dune (and dune files named "dune")
           - skip dirs: _build .git .pi .ralph node_modules
             _opam .opamswitch spikes (optional) supervisor
             (deprecated — skip) image/static/codemirror is ok
           - max file size ~2 MiB
           - GET only; no write/eval

         Default open file: pricklypear.borg when present.

         =========================================================
         BOOK PDF + TARBALL
         =========================================================
         Built at deploy/dev time, not per-request:

           borge book print . -o pricklypear-book
           scripts/public_artifacts.sh  → PP_PUBLIC_DIR/
             book.pdf  (or pricklypear-book.pdf)
             src.tgz   (same allowlist as /code)

         Image serves:
           GET /book.pdf  application/pdf
           GET /src.tgz   application/gzip
         from PP_PUBLIC_DIR (default /tmp/pp-public or beside source).
         Missing artifact → 404 with short HTML hint, not crash.

         =========================================================
         AUTH GATE EXTENSIONS
         =========================================================
         SYSTEM_HARD_PUBLIC gains:
           /welcome /book.pdf /src.tgz
           /code and /code/*
           /assets and /assets/*
           /.well-known/agent.json
           /agent.txt
           (/book is NOT hard-public)
           (/agent is NOT hard-public — private portal)

         Still HARD PRIVATE: / /bindings /edit /repl /data /api
         /admin /branches /types /pages /agent …

         =========================================================
         AGENT DISCOVERY (public bootstrap, no secrets)
         =========================================================
         Remote agents (Agora, pi, curl on a phone) need a front
         door that explains how to talk to the image WITHOUT a
         checked-out repo. Ship two hard-public kernel GETs:

           GET /.well-known/agent.json
             Content-Type: application/json
             Machine contract: name, version, philosophy, auth
             scheme (HTTP Basic / session cookie), eval endpoint
             (POST /api/eval request+response shapes), bootstrap
             first_calls, rules, curl/python examples, related
             public links. NEVER include passwords or tokens.

           GET /agent.txt
             Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
             Same content as prose Markdown/plain instructions a
             freeform agent can ingest wholesale.

         Implementation: kernel handlers in image/lib (stable
         contract), not Nopales routes — discovery must work even
         when seed/route table is empty. base_url may be derived
         from Host / X-Forwarded-* when present, else omitted or
         a relative-friendly note.

         Eval stays hard-private: unauth POST /api/eval → 401.
         Agents supply image credentials (same as /login).

         Anon / → redirect /welcome (product choice for public host).
         Logged-in / → ui/home as today.

         =========================================================
         ENV
         =========================================================
         PP_SOURCE_ROOT  — tree for /code and tarball input
         PP_PUBLIC_DIR   — book.pdf + src.tgz
         PP_LIBS_DIR     — existing; layout+welcome seed from here
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection html-builders
   (doc "The html/ string-builder contract: block-level whitespace discipline (2026-12-30 assessment of ugly rendered HTML)")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-12-30: product pages arrived as one unbroken line — html/tag is pure string-append and nothing crosses the wire with newline discipline. Kernel pure-html pages already indent; the debt was all Nopales-side. Contract below added to the shared builders.|) *)
   (implements libs/std/lib.pp libs/html/lib.pp pure/prims_html.ml pure/prims_pure.ml image/lib/http_core.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune runtest lisp")
    (smoke "a product page (e.g. /welcome) puts block-level tags on their own lines; chrome stays pure-html indented")
    (smoke "no newline appears inside pre/textarea/script/style content (bodies render byte-identical apart from block boundaries)")
    (smoke "inline tags (a span strong code em button label) stay adjacent to surrounding text")
    (smoke "unauth GET /css/layout.css answers 200 text/css with Cache-Control from the ?v= hash match")
    (smoke "unauth GET /css/layout.css?v=<wrong-hash> answers 200 text/css max-age=0")
    (smoke "GET /welcome emits <link rel=stylesheet href=/css/layout.css?v=…> and no layout/theme <style> block"))

   (details
    (doc (|
         =========================================================
         WHY
         =========================================================
         Kernel/system pages (http_core chrome, /branches, /data…)
         are pure-html TREES — pure_html.to_string indents them fine.
         Product pages are Nopales route handlers returning raw HTML
         strings built by the html/ package (html/tag / html/join /
         html/void / layout/*), embedded via txt ~raw:true inside the
         chrome. The string builders concatenate with string-append
         and NEVER emit a newline, so every handler body is one
         unbroken line (measured 2026-12-30 on /welcome: single 6.8KB
         line). This is a pure readability/debuggability tax: view
         source, human and LLM review of rendered output, and
         per-page diffs all suffer. Transfer size is fine; browsers
         collapse the newlines back to nothing.

         =========================================================
         CONTRACT (libs/std — authoritative; libs/html mirrors it)
         =========================================================
         Both packages define the same html/* names; boot seed loads
         std FIRST and is additive (first writer wins), so std's
         definitions are live. The html package vault file must be
         kept byte-equivalent on these names until it is deduped.

         1. BLOCK-LEVEL NEWLINES: (html/tag name attrs body) where
            name is a block-level element emits ONE newline before
            the open tag and ONE newline after the close tag.
            Block set (literal lowercase names): div p h1 h2 h3 h4
            h5 h6 ul ol li dl dt dd table thead tbody tfoot tr
            section header footer main nav article form fieldset
            blockquote figure figcaption aside pre hr.

         2. NOTHING ELSE CHANGES: bodies, attributes, and text
            content are emitted verbatim. The newlines sit only
            OUTSIDE tag boundaries — therefore step 1 can never put
            a newline inside pre/textarea/script/style content
            (no textual-container special case is needed; that is
            the point of placing newlines outside the element).

         3. INLINE STAYS INLINE: names outside the block set (a
            span strong code em button label option input …) render
            exactly as before, adjacent to surrounding text.

         4. RENDERING IS UNCHANGED: block boundaries already break
            lines in the box model, and whitespace at a line start
            or end is stripped, so pages render the same; only
            view-source improves. Adjacent block boundaries may
            produce blank lines — acceptable, not optimized away
            (no reflow pass).

         5. ASSUMPTION: tag names are literal lowercase strings,
            as every existing caller does. No runtime case-folding.

         NON-GOALS (deliberately):
           - NO indentation pass. Correct indent depth would need
             builder state (a drawer) or a re-indent post-pass over
             the wire — v1 contract is newlines-at-block-boundaries
             only. A debug-only pretty renderer (xmllint --html or
             equivalent, never in the serving path) is the escape
             hatch for deep inspection.
           - NO kernel change: pure-html is already indented; the
             chrome is not the ugliness source.
           - NO minification goal. Whitespace is spent for humans;
             transfer is not a v1 constraint.

         =========================================================
         CSS DEBT (assessed 2026-12-30; stylesheet face added
         2026-12-30 — inline sweeps remain)
         =========================================================
         The second half of "ugly" is styling strategy, not string
         building: every product page inlines its CSS —
           - layout/style renders the layout/theme css/render tree
             into an inline <style> per page;
           - neocities-style ships a raw ~5KB style string per page;
           - repl and several product libs hand-concat <style>;
           - theme-* packages stack more style blocks on theme-vars.

         IMPLEMENTED — THE STYLESHEET FACE (layout.css-face):
           A Nopales handler can serve non-HTML bytes from a route:
           (bytes-response content-type max-age body) wraps the value
           in a __bytes__: sentinel (sibling of __html__ /
           __redirect__ / __status__); respond_handler_value answers
           it with text_response + Cache-Control: public, max-age.
           text/html would NOT do for sheets — the chrome sends
           x-content-type-options: nosniff, so mis-typed stylesheets
           are blocked (http_core.csp-invariants).

           layout/* gains:
             (public-route "/css/layout.css" "layout/css")
             layout/css         — face handler: renders
               (css/render (layout/theme)); when ?v= sha1 matches
               the rendered text it answers max-age 31536000
               (immutable) else max-age 0 (re-check)
             layout/css-link    — versioned <link> tag combinator
           layout/page + layout/page-with-css link the face instead
           of inlining (layout/style stays for callers that want the
           inline form; neocities-style stays inline — it is the
           homesite flavor, not product chrome).

         GATE POLICY (branching.public-route, 2026-12-30):
           The gate now reads a handler's BODY (VLambda or VThunk
           cell) WITHOUT forcing — forcing executed handler bodies,
           which raise for request-context prims outside a request.
           The publish-time check is skipped during additive boot
           seed (the env then holds only the kernel prim catalog —
           fingerprint-skipped defines never bind, so the old check
           denied every fresh package's (public-route …) and the
           route row could never store). The ENFORCING gate remains
           per-dispatch: Session.call_handler re-runs
           check_public_handler against the full session env before
           any public handler executes.

           The face path is /css/layout.css, NOT /assets/*: the
           kernel asset Prefix route intercepts /assets/* before the
           user-route catch-all, so a Nopales-registered /assets
           face would 404. /css/* keeps the change kernel-free.

           CACHING CONTRACT: cache-busting is content-addressed —
           the page links ?v=<sha1 of sheet text>; a variant (e.g.
           a personal-branch layout/theme override) renders a
           different hash so per-user variants get separate cache
           entries. After a theme change the page links the new ?v;
           the old URL stays valid forever (immutable) — no purge
           machinery.

         STILL INLINE (debt, sweep per-package on touch):
           - neocities-style (raw ~5KB string per page)
           - notes/tree/style-css (three rendered sheets inline)
           - filmstrip/css, repl, ui style strings
           - inline style= attributes: rb, qmlsmith, waves, forge,
             flash, pads, weight
           New code MUST NOT use inline style= attributes; use
           classes. Theme-* packages: switching themes still means
           swapping which sheet renders; whether theme selection
           compiles into the face or rides a per-user URL variant is
           the next decision (deferred until more packages migrate).
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection analytics
   (doc "Product metrics page: viewers, logins, traffic — observation not work")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: ops_stats + http_ops /analytics + ops/summary prim. Live day rollup; GET /analytics 200.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/ops_stats.ml image/lib/ops_log.ml image/lib/http_ops.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "GET /analytics (authed) shows day rollup: viewers, page views, logins")
    (smoke "(ops/summary) returns structured day stats via /api/eval")
    (smoke "public /welcome increments viewer/page counters; /assets/* excluded")
    (smoke "login success/fail counts match auth_log for the day"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PURPOSE
         A habitat-native analytics page — not Datadog. Answers:
         "who showed up, what did they hit, how many logins?"
         Reliability *counts* appear here; reliability *work*
         lives in notes/todos (see reliability-work-queue).

         SPLIT (product law)
         - /analytics  = observatory (numbers, trends)
         - notes/todos = workshop (errors become fix work)
         Never dump raw log lines into the analytics page as
         the primary UX. Link out to open incident todos.

         EMIT (request edge, one place in HTTP middleware)
         After identity resolve, for each request record:
           day, path_class, method, is_authed, status_bucket,
           viewer_key = hash of (client_ip + day salt) — no raw
           IP in the UI. Exclude /assets/*, /health, static bots
           path classes (wp-admin, .env probes) from uniques or
           bucket as "noise".
         Prefer in-memory day counters flushed periodically;
         do NOT insert one Postgres row per public request.

         AUTH NUMBERS
         Source of truth: existing auth_log (password/mint ok|fail).
         Daily login_ok / login_fail via SQL count, not a second
         write path on POST /login.

         ROLLUP SHAPE (v1)
         In-process Ops_stats for "today" + optional data_rows
         type ops-daily for prior days (fields JSON):
           day, unique_viewers, page_views, logins_ok, logins_fail,
           authed_requests, errors, fatals, top_paths (list).
         Counters flush periodically (in-memory day counters
         plus optional ops-daily rows); there are NO
         per-request Postgres inserts.

         SURFACE
         - GET /analytics  (hard-private; any logged-in user in v1,
           admin-only later if multi-tenant sharpens)
         - Nopales (ops/summary [day]) (ops/top-paths [n])
         - Agent tool ops_summary (read-only)
         - Nav: link from ui/nav under system/admin cluster when
           package ops is seeded (or kernel chrome link)

         UNIQUE VIEWERS (honest labels)
         - public_uniques: distinct viewer_key on hard-public paths
         - logins: successful auth_log password/mint that day
         - active_users: distinct authed usernames that day
         Do not conflate the three in the UI copy.

         NON-GOALS v1
         Session replay, heatmaps, off-box export, per-hit PG
         inserts, GA-style retention cohorts.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection activity-ledger
   (doc "Identity-attributed action ledger, SMF-shaped: who, what class, what target, what outcome, what cost — structure events only, never content. An operator can answer \"who did what, when, at what spend\" from the product instead of the DB. The privacy/auditing doctrine is owned by security.borg §the-watched-machine; this section owns the mechanism.")
   (status implemented)
   (implements migrations/035_activity_ledger.sql image/lib/activity_ledger.ml image/lib/prims_ops.ml image/lib/session.ml image/lib/http.ml image/lib/auth_store.ml image/lib/crud.ml image/lib/prims_auth.ml image/lib/boot.ml image/lib/request_context.ml image/lib/knock_policy.ml image/lib/ops_incidents.ml image/test/activity_ledger_test.ml)
   (* agent note (|2026-10-02: spec'd from the marcus guest-user test + mainframe-SMF discussion (roerick + agent). Heritage: function_statring (031) already proves the storage shape (dedicated table, ring, async flush, set-based prune — TELEMETRY IS NOT CONTENT), auth_log (002) proves audit rows, tree_versions proves attribution, per-eval budgets prove the cost fields; the missing pieces are the by-ACTOR read faces, the deterministic knock policy, and the operator console.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "(ops/activity [actor] [since] [class]) returns rows with actor/class/target/outcome/cost; the class is a validated grammar (safe_sort_sql precedent), an unknown class rejects")
    (smoke "an unauthenticated /api/eval denial, a login, and a token mint each land as ledger rows with outcome=denied|ok|ok; source_ip present on auth.* rows only")
    (smoke "labeled evals push api.eval rows carrying steps/prim_calls/db_hits — asynchronous, ZERO synchronous DB work on the request path (031 pattern)")
    (smoke "033-disjunct rescue of a data.u.<other-user> row emits admin.content-read with author, target, session; the lonely-habitat bit (one human user) suppresses the class entirely")
    (smoke "N denials on admin surfaces in a window files exactly ONE ops-incident (fingerprint dedup), a security severity class distinct from reliability; LLM enrich may rewrite title/body, never counts")
    (smoke "(ops/activity) unfiltered for admin; a non-admin caller is force-filtered to its own actor rows")
    (smoke "IKEA test: day rollups per (actor, class) with steps/db_ms sums survive a 365-day run; trifecta row computed from class counts only")
   )
   (details
    (doc (|
         PURPOSE
         A habitat running agents needs an operator who can see.
         The 2026-10-02 marcus test proved the gap: the data
         existed (auth_log rows, function_stat.author,
         tree_versions) but no product surface could answer
         "when did he log in, what is he running, what is it
         costing, what did he touch" — the answer required
         psql. The ledger is the SMF analog: one structured,
         typed, identity-attributed event stream that stays
         OFF the request path and OFF the object tree.

         THE SPLIT (extends the product law — a THIRD plane)
         - /analytics = product metrics: aggregate, identity-
           stripped, hashed viewer keys — exactly as shipped;
           its NON-GOALS keep binding it.
         - ledger     = tenant/operator activity: attributed,
           admin-gated, structure-only, bounded retention.
         - notes/todos = the workshop, unchanged; knock fixtures
           and incident work items live here.
         The two data planes never merge.

         RECORD (migration 034; strictly additive; the 031
         pattern — TELEMETRY IS NOT CONTENT — is the reason)
         Dedicated table, ring-buffered push (bounded, drop-
         oldest in-process), ~30s background flush fiber, set-
         based retention prune (newest ~50k raw rows), and day
         rollup rows per (actor, class) with steps/db_ms sums
         kept forever (chargeback substrate; budgets-as-security
         gets its per-identity numbers). Columns:
           (id, at, actor, session_id, branch_id,
            class,   -- a VALIDATED enum grammar, never free
                      -- text (safe_sort_sql precedent):
                      -- auth.login|mint|revoke|deny|delegated
                      -- api.eval · route.hit · tree.write ·
                      -- data.write · rls.violation ·
                      -- admin.content-read|hold|knock|revoke|
                      -- create|delete · promote.attempt ·
                      -- token.mint · agent.spawn ·
                      -- egress.http · quota.exceeded
            target,  -- a PATH (b.fn.* / data.* / /route),
                      -- never a body, never a payload
            outcome, -- ok | denied | error | timed-out
            cost)    -- steps, prim_calls, db_hits, db_ms —
                      -- the budget object already carried by
                      -- every eval (budget-ledger heritage)
         source_ip is carried on auth.* classes only (auth_log
         parity); it is not recorded on ordinary page/eval hits.
         No RLS on the ledger — the reader prims gate it
         (admin sees all, non-admin sees self).

         EMIT — existing seams, no new hot paths
         - http.ml record_request (the one request funnel)
           → route.hit rows
         - Session.eval_string_rim_budgeted, where
           Function_stats.push already sits (its exact budget
           object, its author) → api.eval rows; function_stat
           stays the by-fn perf view, the ledger is the by-ACTOR
           view of the same event
         - Auth_store.log_auth (already written) is the
           auth.* source of truth — the ledger mirrors, never
           forks the write
         - tree/datum upserts → tree.write / data.write / and
           rls.violation rows (the 013/033 floors brand these)
         - admin action handlers (issue-token, revoke-sessions,
           create-user, delete-user, cancel-job, package-disable,
           set-user-type, set-allow-agent-promote) → admin.*
         - THE 033 HOOK: when the admin disjunct rescues a row
           owner-scoping would have hidden, emit
           admin.content-read — deliberate access is an
           attributed act (RACF's type-80 on admin opens, in
           reverse)
         - a lonely-habitat bit (PP_LONELY_HABITAT or exactly
           one human user) skips the content-read ceremony

         READ — the operator console (filmstrip, not a new page-style)
         - (ops/activity [actor] [since] [class]): admin-
         unfiltered; non-admin callers force-filtered to self —
         a watched machine shows its watch to the watched; a
         user's own activity face is honest UX. Read-only, safe
         for agent monitors.
         - The console ships as a filmstrip strip: live
           sessions from Session_registry, a live ledger tail,
           the EXISTING views (agent-sessions transcripts,
           /timing numbers, ops/incidents) drilled from rows,
           per-identity spend/burst from day rollups, and the
           HOLD verbs (revoke token / cancel job / disable
           package) one click from the feed. Mainframe
           operator's batch-hold, on a Lisp shell.
         - Trifecta row: per identity, computed from CLASS
           COUNTS ONLY — does this actor simultaneously hold
         private-data reads, untrusted-content ingest, and an
         effect channel (lethal-trifecta, made computable).
         Flag when all three legs are live.

         OPERATOR FAST-PATH (2026-10-02 marcus recon — the
         acceptance bar for the read faces; each of these
         burned ten-plus minutes of prim-probing today)
         - WHO IS ON, and when did each identity last
           authenticate, from where — the auth_log read face;
           today login provenance is psql-only.
         - WHAT IS X RUNNING RIGHT NOW — attributed, labeled
           eval stream; the 2,674 /api hits of 2026-10-02
           could not be attributed to identities AT ALL.
         - WHAT DID X WRITE TODAY, ANYWHERE — one query, all
           branches; today mirrors and readers disagree on
           scope, so the honest answer needed five different
           prims (see branching.borg branch-resolved-reads
           SCOPE GRAMMAR)
         - WHAT DID X TRY THAT WAS DENIED OR FAILED — the
           silent-write surface; marcus's agent's vanished
           /issues attempt is the repro, and today the two
         cases "never tried" and "tried and evaporated" are
         indistinguishable. outcome + rls.violation rows
         make the distinction visible — and the console
         carries the branch-resolved body reads so suspected
         defs open in one click, no checkout.

         KNOCK SCHEMA (deterministic — LAW 1)
         Thresholds + classes, never content, never vibes: N
         denials on admin surfaces in a window · rls.violation
         repeats · non-admin token.mint / promote.attempt ·
         first-login-from-new-IP · cost outliers vs the
         identity's own 7-day median · trifecta all-flags-up.
         Files through the EXISTING ops_incidents fingerprint
         queue (dup bumps count, no second todo) under a
         security severity class so it sorts apart from
         reliability. A human sends the knock (chat DM for a
         human, a hold for a process); the act writes
         admin.knock / admin.hold like every other deliberate
         operator act. Nothing fires itself. LLM may enrich a
         filed incident's title/body; never counts, never
         detection.

         NON-GOALS
         - No content in, adjacent to, or derivable from the
           ledger: no eval source, no route/note/chat bodies,
           no payloads — target paths only. TELEMETRY IS NOT
           CONTENT, and this table is exactly why that rule
           exists (the 031 admission: a 25-minute retention
           delete took prod down TWICE).
         - No content-keyed automation (security.borg LAW 3).
         - No synchronous per-hit writes (031 pattern, ring).
         - No session replay, no ML anomaly scoring, no
           keystroke fishing.
         - Off-box SIEM export deferred (matches the analytics
           non-goals); syslog/stderr mirroring of warn+
           ledger events is the interim export path.
         - No hosted crypto-floor tier (security.borg
           §the-watched-machine): sovereignty via §exit is the
           privacy mechanic, not operator blindness.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection reliability-work-queue
   (doc "Structured errors → fingerprint → note + todo; LLM enriches work items")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: ops_log + ops_incidents fingerprint→note/todo; log-tail + ops/incidents. LLM enrich optional later.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/ops_log.ml image/lib/ops_incidents.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "(log-tail 20) returns recent structured events")
    (smoke "Error-level log creates/upserts one open incident fingerprint")
    (smoke "repeat same error bumps count, does not create duplicate todo")
    (smoke "incident filing creates a note body + open todo-item")
    (smoke "optional LLM enrich rewrites title/body without changing counts"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PURPOSE
         Errors should become work. The habitat already is notes +
         todos; reliability must enter that queue, not only sit on
         an ops dashboard. Analytics shows counts; this subsection
         turns severe/new failures into fixable garden items.

         STRUCTURED LOG (primary stream)
         Ops_log ring in the image process:
           {ts, level, component, msg, context alist, fingerprint}
         Levels: info | warn | error | fatal.
         Always mirror to stderr (/var/log/pricklypear/image.log)
         for operator crash forensics. Bounded ring (e.g. 5k).
         Closed-writer / handler / seed failures go through Log,
         not bare Printf.eprintf forever (migrate hot sites).

         FINGERPRINT
         key = component + "|" + normalized msg head (strip UUIDs,
         paths ids). One open incident per key. Upsert:
           count++, last_seen, sample_msg, sample_context.
         New key OR fatal OR watchdog restart → file work item.
         Bot 404 noise stays analytics-only (warn or ignored).

         WORK ITEM SHAPE
         1. data_rows type ops-incident (machine state):
              fingerprint, level, component, count, first_seen,
              last_seen, status (open|resolved), note_slug,
              todo_id, sample_msg
         2. notes: durable human/agent readable writeup
              slug ops-incident-<shortfp> or capture under inbox
         3. todo-item: open TODO linked in notes field to the note
         Dedup rule: if open ops-incident exists for fingerprint,
         bump counts only — do not mint a second todo.

         LLM ROLE (enrichment, not detection)
         Detection = fingerprints + thresholds (deterministic).
         LLM optional pass:
           input = fingerprint + samples + counts + recent log-tail
           output = better title, explanation, suggested fix/repro
         Write into the note body / todo title. Never invent counts.
         If LLM down: still file terse template note+todo.
         Trigger: on new incident, or admin "Enrich" / hourly batch
         for open incidents lacking ai_enriched flag.

         CLOSE THE LOOP
         status → resolved when:
           - todo marked DONE, or
           - no matching error for N hours (optional auto),
           - admin resolves on /analytics
         Resolved incidents do not re-open on single blip; require
         count threshold or fatal to reopen.

         PRIMS / AGENT
         (log-tail n [level])
         (ops/incidents [status])
         (ops/file-incident ...)  ; mostly internal
         Agent tools: read_logs, ops_summary, list_incidents (read);
         write tools stay effect-gated as today.

         WATCHDOG
         OpenBSD cron watchdog remains the process safety net
         (scripts/openbsd/pricklypear-watchdog.ksh). On restart it
         may log fatal via syslog; image boot records a fatal/info
         "boot after downtime" event when possible. Watchdog is
         outside the image; do not pretend Nopales can restart itself
         after a hard process death.

         V1 CUT (shipped)
         - Ops_log ring + stderr mirror
         - Ops_stats request counters + auth_log login counts
         - GET /analytics page (kernel)
         - Fingerprint store in data_rows ops-incident
         - Auto-file note+todo on new error/fatal
         - Template body first; LLM enrich if Ai path available
         - (log-tail) (ops/summary) prims
         Defer: multi-week charts, bot ML, off-box shipping.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection horizon
   (doc "Long-term possible futures built on this substrate (design only)")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: design-only futures doc; implemented as spec-complete.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Possible futures (not commitments):

         A. Personal computing environment
            Always-on personal image; notes/CRM/automations
            as live functions; browser as desktop.

         B. Multi-user community OS
            system/main commons; personal garden branches;
            merge reviews; author-runs-as-author capabilities.

         C. AI-native software workshop
            AI cohabits image; proposes types+fns+routes as
            app sketches; binding-level diffs; provenance;
            later addressable AST patches.

         D. Internal tools platform
            Team branches; shared business objects; live
            inspectable tools without redeploy for Nopales-tier
            changes.

         E. Education / research lab
            Legible multi-user systems + PL teaching with
            forks/merges and visible isolation.

         F. End-user programmable products
            Friendly skin on same substrate; templates as
            starter branches; AI drafts, users edit.

         G. Hardened multi-tenant service
            Solo5, real SSH + web login, argon2, mTLS,
            fleet policies, true setuid data scoping.

         H. Dabar — live Bible study libraries
            BSB reader + Strong's + interlinear as load-library
            packages (bible/ + dabar/) on the image; not a
            separate OS. Spec: dabar.borg (root-inlined chapter).
            Semantic search deferred.

         Milestone ladder:
           now   — hacker env + browser login + Nopales product shell
                   (define/edit/route/AI; ui/nav ui/home; auth session)
           next  — product theme Path B (css/* + ui/theme); kill
                   silent OCaml product defaults (nav/home/CSS);
                   emergency-only chrome+css; shell denser; branch
                   promote UI
           later — environment feel (more system UI in Nopales,
                   permissions, multi-user fleet isolation)
           horizon — community + AI coworker + Solo5 product

         Branch UX + software updates: see branching.borg
         subsections personal-workspace (implemented),
         package-updates / promote / branch-browser-ui
         (planned). Defaults on system/main; garden on
         <user>/personal; promote is selective merge + git.

         Principles to preserve:
           live system over file tree; store is truth;
           code branches / data shared; functions compose;
           AI is an author not god mode; inspectability;
           isolation by process.
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
